ecause Chi requires consecutive numbers. W6, W7, W8. Do I have W6? No. \n\nAlternatively, W7, W8, W9. If I can get W9, that's a Chi. \n\nBut I have W7 and W8. If I can get W9, that's a Chi. \n\nSo maybe I should keep W8 in hand to try to complete that. \n\nLooking at the unshown tiles: W9 has 2 remaining. \n\nIf I play W8, I might lose the chance to get W9 for the Chi. \n\nAlternatively, if I play W8, maybe someone else will take it, but if I keep it, I can try to complete the Chi later. \n\nAnother angle: the melds I have are in the T suit. Maybe I can form another Chi in the T suit. I have T3 and T4. If I get T5, that's a Chi. But T5 is only 2 remaining. \n\nAlternatively, T4 and T7... Not helpful. \n\nLooking at the W suit: W1 and W2. If I can get W3, that's a Chi. But W3 has 3 remaining. \n\nSo possible options for melds: \n\n- Chi W1-W2-W3 (if I get W3) \n\n- Chi W7-W8-W9 (if I get W9) \n\n- Chi T3-T4-T5 (if I get T5) \n\n- Pung of W8 (if I get another W8) \n\n- Pung of F3 (if I get another F3) \n\nNow, considering the current hand and the valid actions, which tiles can I play? The valid actions are Play W7, W1, F3, W4, W2, T3, W8, T4, T7. \n\nSo I can play any of those. \n\nIf I play F3, that would reduce my F3 count to 1, making it harder to form a Pung. But maybe I need to discard to build a better hand. \n\nAlternatively, if I play W8, I lose a chance to form a Pung or a Chi. \n\nBut let's think about the current points. \n\nI need to reach at least 8 points. Let's see what I have. \n\nIf I can form a Pung of F3s (three of them), that's a Dragon Pung (2 points). If I have two Pungs, that's 4 points. \n\nIf I can form a Chi of W7-W8-W9, that's part of a Pure Straight (16 points) if I have three chows in the same suit. \n\nBut I need to check if I can form that. \n\nAlternatively, if I can form a Pure Straight with three chows in W suit. For example, if I have W1-W2-W3, W4-W5-W6, W7-W8-W9. But I only have W1, W2, W4, W7, W8. So missing W3, W5, W6, W9. \n\nNot